Compute the immediate-type index into a local before indexing.

mp_obj_get_type ends with `types[(uintptr_t)o_in & 0xf]`. Written inline, the widening of that
4-bit index to the array subscript's pointer-width index type sinks into the mask, so the AND is
performed at POINTER width -- and reading the address of an operand that wide needs a
cursor-read instruction, which raises on an untagged value. Every immediate object word reaching
this line is untagged by construction (a small int, a qstr, None, a bool), so it faults on the
first call. Verified in the emitted code: the instruction is present before this change and
absent after it.

A named local forces the mask to be computed and materialised at integer width first. On a
target with a single pointer width this is the same code.
diff --git a/py/obj.c b/py/obj.c
index 252e7372f..6dcf58304 100644
--- a/py/obj.c
+++ b/py/obj.c
@@ -66,7 +66,16 @@ const mp_obj_type_t *MICROPY_WRAP_MP_OBJ_GET_TYPE(mp_obj_get_type)(mp_const_obj_
             NULL, &mp_type_int, &mp_type_str, &mp_type_int,
             NULL, &mp_type_int, &mp_type_bool, &mp_type_int,
         };
-        return types[(uintptr_t)o_in & 0xf];
+        /* The index goes through an explicit size_t local rather than being written inline.
+           Inline, the widening of the 4-bit index to the GEP's pointer-width index type sinks
+           into the mask, so the AND is performed at CAPABILITY width -- and reading the address
+           of an operand that width needs `lcc`, which raises on an untagged value. Every
+           immediate object word here is untagged by construction, so that faults on the first
+           call. A local forces the mask to be computed and materialised at integer width
+           first. No behaviour change anywhere; on a target with one pointer width it is the
+           same code. */
+        size_t type_idx = (size_t)((uintptr_t)o_in & 0xf);
+        return types[type_idx];
     }
 
     #elif MICROPY_OBJ_IMMEDIATE_OBJS && MICROPY_OBJ_REPR == MICROPY_OBJ_REPR_C
